{"name":"Blue Sari","hex":"#666a76","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"sari","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The traditional dress of women in the Indian Subcontinent; an outer garment consisting of a single length of cotton or silk, most often with one end wrapped around the waist to form a skirt, the other draped over the shoulder or head."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sari"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sari","blocks":[{"text":"A sari is a draped cloth and women's garment in the Indian subcontinent. It consists of an un-stitched stretch of woven fabric arranged over the body as a dress, with one end attached to the waist, while the other end rests over one shoulder as a stole, sometimes baring a part of the midriff. It may vary from 4.5 to 9 yards in length, and 24 to 47 inches in breadth, and is a form of ethnic clothing in India, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Sri Lanka, and Nepal.There are various names and styles of sari manufacture and draping, the most common being the Nivi style. The sari is worn with a fitted bodice also called a choli and a petticoat called ghagra, parkar, or ul-pavadai. It remains fashionable in the Indian subcontinent and is also considered formal attire in the countries of the region."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Sari_on_mannequin_for_demo.jpg/330px-Sari_on_mannequin_for_demo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sari"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Maharani_Vijaya_Raje_Scindia_of_Gwalior%2C_ca.1940.jpg/500px-Maharani_Vijaya_Raje_Scindia_of_Gwalior%2C_ca.1940.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Maharani_Vijaya_Raje_Scindia_of_Gwalior%2C_ca.1940.jpg/500px-Maharani_Vijaya_Raje_Scindia_of_Gwalior%2C_ca.1940.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Maharani Vijaya Raje Scindia of Gwalior dressed in sari, c. 1940","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaharani_Vijaya_Raje_Scindia_of_Gwalior%2C_ca.1940.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/%E0%A4%9F%E0%A5%87%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%95%E0%A5%8B%E0%A4%9F%E0%A4%BE_%E0%A4%AF%E0%A4%95%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%B7%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%A3%E0%A5%80.jpg/500px-%E0%A4%9F%E0%A5%87%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%95%E0%A5%8B%E0%A4%9F%E0%A4%BE_%E0%A4%AF%E0%A4%95%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%B7%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%A3%E0%A5%80.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 